<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyTitle>Quartz.NET Dashboard Agent</AssemblyTitle>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Title>Quartz.NET Dashboard Agent</Title>
    <Description>Quartz.NET dashboard agent - a plugin that dials a scheduler out to a Quartz dashboard over one outbound connection, so the dashboard can show and drive it without an inbound port; $(Description)</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);dashboard;agent;signalr;remote;fleet</PackageTags>
    <RootNamespace>Quartz</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      The claim, and what it is worth - the same statement Quartz.csproj makes, and read the same way.
      IsAotCompatible turns on IsTrimmable and the trim, AOT and single-file analyzers, all of which were
      already on here; what it adds is the statement, so the four are spelled out beside it.

      What is proven: Quartz.Trimming.Canary.Wire references this package, is published by ILCompiler as a
      native executable on Windows, Linux and macOS on every pull request, and is run. The hub half of the
      protocol lives in Quartz.Dashboard, which Blazor keeps untrimmable, so the canary carries a hub of its
      own speaking the dashboard's half with the contract's own records, and a scheduler dials out to it
      through UseDashboardAgent: it registers, heartbeats, answers a read, a schedule naming a job type as a
      string, a trigger with the event stream open, a refusal of its own and a 404, and says goodbye - the
      SignalR client's On<T>, InvokeAsync<T> and client-to-server streaming binding every payload through
      the wire contract's generated metadata, with reflection-based System.Text.Json switched off.
      Quartz.Examples.Worker publishes it fully trimmed as well, and never starts it.

      What the baseline still records, and a native publish still reports: one IL2026, the one call site the
      HTTP API has too - a job type named as a string in a request body - in TrimAnalysisBaseline.cs beside
      this file. It is trimming, not dynamic code: the package produces no IL3050 at all.

      Where it stops: the claim is this assembly's. Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client underneath it says
      IsAotCompatible for itself, and a strongly typed hub - the one SignalR shape native AOT does not
      support - is nowhere in this package.
    -->
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The SignalR client and nothing of the server: no Microsoft.AspNetCore.App, so a worker that is a plain
      generic host takes no web framework to be shown on a dashboard.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.SignalR.Client" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz\Quartz.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
